t-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","key":"oLz8xsyJWn"},{"type":"text","value":"), our test statistic has a t distribution with (n − 1) d.f., and this is the distribution under which we find p-values.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"HcvTUdHcqR"}],"key":"GSQMXTd9Zy"}],"key":"rpnLVjgU2H"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a large sample size (n), the null distribution of the test statistic is approximately Z, so whether we use t(n − 1) or Z to calculate the p-values should not make a big difference. Here is another practical way to look at this point: if we have a large n, our sample has more information about the population. Therefore, we can expect the sample standard deviation s to be close enough to the population standard deviation σ that for practical purposes we can use s as the known σ, and we’re back to the z-test.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"TRe8u5Q9Lc"}],"key":"s3pI12txJj"}],"key":"X3nOqeJOiT"}],"key":"TVXiUR6nCe"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Concept Check","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"tTkdVnP2mb"}],"identifier":"concept-check","label":"Concept Check","html_id":"concept-check","implicit":true,"key":"vmHKHllIA8"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-e9689e083a3d48b3e46875a14efdf8d0.mjs","model":{"question":"A t-test for a mean is based on a sample of n = 15. How many degrees of freedom does the null distribution of the test statistic have?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"14","feedback":"Correct! The t-test statistic has n − 1 = 14 degrees of freedom."},{"correct":false,"text":"15","feedback":"The degrees of freedom are one LESS than the sample size."},{"correct":false,"text":"2","feedback":"The degrees of freedom depend on the sample size, not the number of hypotheses."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Degrees of freedom = n − 1.","explanation":""},"key":"EJ0R3z3Hsr"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-e9689e083a3d48b3e46875a14efdf8d0.mjs","model":{"question":"Why does using s instead of σ change the null distribution from Z to t?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Using s adds a second source of sample-to-sample variability, giving the statistic more spread than a z-score","feedback":"Correct! s varies from sample to sample, adding a second source of variability on top of x-bar's, which fattens the tails of the distribution of the statistic."},{"correct":false,"text":"Because s is always larger than σ","feedback":"s is not systematically larger than σ—it varies around it."},{"correct":false,"text":"Because using s introduces a calculation error","feedback":"The change affects small samples most, but the reason is the extra variability, not a calculation error."}],"multi":false,"hint":"How many randomly varying quantities appear in each formula?","explanation":""},"key":"TbjyLFYse5"}],"key":"K4NuY6pZUp"}],"key":"BYlN1meIoo"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"When σ Is Unknown: The t-Test and t Distribution","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mean-6-o","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"},"next":{"title":"Finding the P-value with the t Distribution","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mean-8-o","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}